WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.380
If I do my job in the next few minutes
I'm going to give you the secret to happiness

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.889
so that when you go home tonight you can
start living a happier life

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:12.280
and you can start sharing with more
people so that they can have

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.980
a happier life too. So let's start with
what we know about happiness.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.760
There have been hundreds, literally hundreds, of
studies

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.390
by economists and social scientists and
psychologists about happiness,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:29.470
hundreds over the past thirty and forty
years. And basically they all start by

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:30.840
asking the question: who's happy?

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.860
In the United States we know that all
the way back to the 1970s a

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.450
very stable proportion of the population is
very happy.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.129
About a third of Americans say
anonymously

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:45.070
and in accurate tests an accurate surveys
that they're very happy people.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:49.200
Okay, we have no reason to doubt that this
is true. A little over fifty percent say

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.570
they're somewhat happy,

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:54.390
about 10 to 15 percent of the population
is chronically depressed and say they're

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.190
not very happy.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:58.520
Okay now, that's actually not the most
interesting question. The most

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.750
interesting question is what brings
happiness.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:05.260
And we know the answer to that too. It's
three things,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.700
three things bring happiness: genetics,

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.880
big life events, and choices.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.960
And I'm going to tell you about all three,
starting with genetics.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.909
Now, a few years ago social scientists at
the University of Minnesota

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:24.080
constructed a wonderful database that
included seventy five pairs of identical

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.180
twins born between the mid-1930s and the mid 1950s.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.509
Now they were separated at birth and
adopted by separate families.

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:38.400
It's like a social scientist's dream
it's almost as if the government had done this.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.350
And at age 40 they were reunited and
they were given a personality test, so

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:47.700
they were asked about the things that were
going on their lives and the way that they

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.520
viewed their lives.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.540
This is really interesting because
you've got DNA carbon copies

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.479
of each other and they have different
upbringings so you can net out the parts

00:01:56.479 --> 00:01:59.130
that are nature and the parts that are
nurture, statistically, and what did they find?

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:04.939
They found that 48 percent of their happiness is
genetic. As a matter of fact they found that more

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:08.599
of their personality was genetic than they
ever understood before. Forty percent of

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:09.910
how you vote is genetic

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.150
forty percent of how you worship is
genetic, so

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.020
this explains some things. Do you have
someone in your life who is

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:21.270
chronically happy? Do you have a coworker who's
happy all the time?

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:26.900
It's very irritating, by the way, and so it
sometimes you wonder, that person is

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.310
just happy all the time.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.190
What are they doing right? What's their
secret? Their secret is

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.610
their DNA. Are you a grumpy person are you

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:40.819
dark and brooding. It's your DNA it's not
your fault, you have lousy parents

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:45.160
is basically what it comes down to. So if
that's the case

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.030
does it really matter what we do? The answer is
yeah because if half of it is taken away

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.900
from you you better get the other half right.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.040
Now forty-percent is genetic but there are
other innate qualities, other innate

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.659
characteristics that are really
important,

00:02:57.659 --> 00:03:00.800
the most interesting of which is gender.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.060
Who's happier, men or women? The answer is
women.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.420
Most studies, the overwhelming majority
of studies, find that women are happier

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:10.790
than men and always have been.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:15.069
Okay I mean it's too bad guys but I
studied this myself and I find the same

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:19.310
thing. I've looked at the
happiness of men and women by marital status.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:23.760
K and what do we find? Married women are happier
than married men,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:30.019
single women are happier than single men,
widowed women are way happier than widowed men.

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:36.120
What's so funny about that? 

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.129
I told that one to my wife

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:43.430
and she says, "Oh, no kidding."

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.549
In general women are happier than men.

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:50.209
Here's another interesting mystery:
what's the average

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:56.000
unhappiest age in a man's life? I'd heard
about this phenomenon forever, so have you,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.239
this is the male mid-life crisis, right?
When do you think it is?

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:02.390
70, 17

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:05.790
That doesn't count, only grown ups. 45

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:11.420
45 it's 45 that is exactly right. 45 is the
average unhappiest age in a man's life.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:12.790
And it goes something like this:

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.130
the trajectory of life for men is
interesting and

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.340
sometimes a little sad. See, when you're
in your twenties

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.580
and thirties the life recipe is
actually kind of simple.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.580
Life is like a super highway when
you're in your twenties and thirties

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.520
if you want to get ahead and you want to have a
better life and you want to be happier and more successful

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.620
just hit the gas. Just look for more
money

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.669
and look for a better career and look
for more promotions and it's simple.

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:43.770
The problem is on the superhighway guys
get to age 45

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.780
and a bunch of them stop and they say

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.910
"I think I'm on the road. I don't want to be on
the superhighway, but

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.870
I can't stop because I've got the house
payment and I've got all this stuff.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:59.380
It's hard, I'll get hit or get in an
accident if I stop, it'll be bad

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:02.440
if I stop, a whole lot of guys find.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.289
Now, ok, next question: what do they want?

00:05:07.289 --> 00:05:11.419
Staying on the same metaphor, what they
want is to be on another road

00:05:11.419 --> 00:05:15.630
that they picked themselves they want to
be on a little dirt road

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:21.389
with a motorcycle on it where the
driver is not wearing a helmet.

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:26.930
That's what they want so who's the guy
on the motorcycle because the secret to happiness

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:30.630
is being the guy on a motorcycle without
the helmet. So I want to know who he is.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:34.930
Tell me about it. I know who he is. He is me.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:38.949
And I'll tell you how it works. When I
was 19,

00:05:38.949 --> 00:05:42.970
I dropped out of college. Dropped out, kicked
out,

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:46.310
splitting hairs.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:49.990
I ran off to play music for a living.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:54.680
I was a French horn player, that was all I wanted
to do. I wound up in the Barcelona symphony

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.669
by my my late twenties. It was great, I
love the music, I still love the music,

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:03.710
it's the best music ever written. Seems like
a dream, right?

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.680
But I got a glimpse of my forty five
year old self.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.050
I got to look in the crystal ball, it was
all the guys working with me,

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:15.389
who were usually 20-30 years older than me,
who around 45 had

00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:19.500
kind of lost their happiness and now they
were drinking a little too much and their

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.580
marriages weren't so good and they didn't like the
music so much.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.680
And I said I don't want to be those guys, so I got
lucky.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.970
Because I saw it and I hatched a plan, I hatched a
plan to

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:33.569
to escape and I decided to go back to college.

00:06:33.569 --> 00:06:37.270
And I called my dad and I said, "Dad I got big news! 
I'm going to quit music.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:42.110
I'm going to go back to college. I'm going to study, I
don't know, literature, physics,

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:45.520
poetry, or economics or something. It's going to
be great.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.020
I'm going to use my brain. My dad said

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:53.900
"What are you talking about? You
started a family,

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:57.460
you're doing fine, it's very
irresponsible to just

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.610
quit when you're in your late twenties, why?"

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:04.780
I said, "Dad, because I'm not happy.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:08.020
And I don't think I'm going to be happy. 

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:12.460
And there's a long pause and he says 
"So what makes you so special?"

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:20.380
Today I'm doing what I love. Today I'm
doing what I feel like I'm called to do.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:24.610
Today my job is to talk to you about how
to have a happier life.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:29.740
What a miracle. Now it's not good enough
for people to accidentally find the way

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:33.880
we need to find it on purpose. Okay so innate
differences:

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:37.500
genetics, forty-eight percent. The next
big one

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.990
is big life events. This is what you
think really drives your happiness.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:44.810
You want to be happy? Go for your goals. You
got these big goals in life and avoid

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:46.349
the bad stuff, right?

00:07:46.349 --> 00:07:49.770
You think about it all the time. If only I
can graduate from college with a good

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.229
enough GPA to get a good job, then I'll be
happy,

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:56.759
right? If only I get into law school then I'll
be happy if only that girl will marry me

00:07:56.759 --> 00:08:01.199
then I'll be happy, and if I don't get sick
I won't be unhappy,

00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:04.319
right? If I don't have an accident I won't
be unhappy. We think

00:08:04.319 --> 00:08:07.500
in this way we are very goal-oriented
creatures.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.560
Now how much of our life happiness at any
particular point

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.810
comes from the big things good and bad?
Answer is

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:20.410
forty-percent. That's a lot. It seems like you're
absolutely right to be chasing the big

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:23.190
thing in any particular moment, trying to
avoid the big bad thing

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.910
at any particular moment but that's
wrong. It's forty percent

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:30.129
but I'm going to tell you why not to chase it.

00:08:30.129 --> 00:08:33.690
Two reasons: number one is we're very bad

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.830
at getting the things that we're seeking,
the big goals that we're seeking,

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:40.899
but more importantly the big things
never last.

00:08:40.899 --> 00:08:44.730
How long will your mood be improved if you get
that dream house?

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.930
Six months. If I double your pay, how
long

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:52.470
will it improve your mood? Up to six
months, no more.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.850
No matter what you do it's going to wear off,
the big stuff wears off.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:58.899
But the costs of doing these big things
last forever.

00:08:58.899 --> 00:09:02.130
Don't chase the big thing. There's
one study that really points this out.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.240
The most interesting study I've ever seen.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.180
From 1978 actually it's an old study, but it's
still classic.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:12.490
And it compares two groups of people:
quadriplegics,

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:16.089
and people who win the lottery. What do
these people have in common?

00:09:16.089 --> 00:09:19.700
A huge punctuation to the equilibrium
BOM

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:23.850
bad or good right? Now quadriplegics

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:27.870
six months after their accidents, on
average, their happiness has returned

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.860
almost to its level prior to their
accident.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.100
That's what the study shows. Now not all the way but
almost all the way.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.940
See how durable we are? Six months later they
would they still say I would do almost anything

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.750
to get to be able to control my arms and
legs to not be confined to a wheelchair,

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:47.820
but their happiness is something that is
innate,

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.010
and has to do with other forces. Okay,
that's fine study number one,

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:55.750
number two is lottery winners. This one's
actually even a little bit more interesting.

00:09:55.750 --> 00:10:00.269
Six months after you hit the lottery the
average lottery winner

00:10:00.269 --> 00:10:03.680
has permanent baseline happiness levels
that are slightly

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.579
below where they were the day before
they bought the ticket.

00:10:08.579 --> 00:10:12.769
Literally the best thing that can happen
to you when you buy a lottery ticket

00:10:12.769 --> 00:10:16.680
is that you don't win. Why? We know this
too.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.940
It's because they've blown their circuits.
The little things in life

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.029
don't make them happy. Think about what is
going to make you happy this weekend.

00:10:24.029 --> 00:10:27.110
Hanging out with loved ones, it's doing
the things that

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.700
give you a little bit of gratification
with the people who make you happy.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:31.329
You're going to watch some sports,

00:10:31.329 --> 00:10:35.860
you probably go out to dinner, you're
going to be around people who you're

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:39.209
comfortable with and you're going to joke
around, have a great time.

00:10:39.209 --> 00:10:42.810
Turns out people who win the lottery don't
get that anymore. All of their hobbies

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:46.730
are dross their friendships are all
different they don't enjoy the little

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:47.920
things they used to enjoy anymore.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:51.459
So they go in search for more flavor. The
truth is

00:10:51.459 --> 00:10:56.529
if you want to be happy, don't spend your time
obsessing about the great big splash.

00:10:56.529 --> 00:11:00.870
What you need are habits that will give you
the highest likelihood of getting that

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:01.750
forty percent

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:04.890
that will drive these events most likely
into your life

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:08.100
and drive the bad things out of your life
in a regular

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:11.120
framework as a regular type of discipline.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:14.899
So what are these lifestyle elements?

00:11:14.899 --> 00:11:18.839
And this brings us to the last 12
percent remember forty-eight is genetics,

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:23.320
40 percent is events, you got 12 percent
left, that 12 percent you have left

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.410
